    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1389">

                <pb facs="1122" n="1112" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg1112"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.714/10–1849: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Peiping</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Clubb</hi>)<anchor n="31" xml:id="d1389-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-27T19:00:00-05:00">October 27, 1949—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>746. Urtel 1769 Oct. 18. In consideration our political relations with Chi Govt,
                    denotation carried by name “Peking” and administrative <pb facs="1123" n="1113"
                        xml:id="pg1113"/> complexities involved, no change being made in
                    Departmental and general FonServ use of “Peiping”. Dept perceives no objection,
                    however, to your following local usage this matter and employing in local
                    communications name of city as designated by local auths. For urinfo, Dept will
                    also employ name “Peking” in any open mail or telegraphic communications
                    transmitted to you via commercial facilities.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1389-f1" n="31" type="source" xml:id="d1389-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to the Embassy at Nanking as No. 1195, to the Chargé in China as telCan
                    No. 738, to the Consul General at Shanghai as No. 2180, and to the Consul
                    General at Tientsin as No. 376.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
